DGL Wayland backend -- planned, not yet implemented
===================================================

This file is a placeholder. The Wayland backend sources do not exist yet, and
dgl/src/pugl.cpp deliberately raises

    #error "DGL Wayland backend not yet implemented, see dgl/src/pugl-extra/README.wayland"

when a build selects the Wayland arm. Hitting that #error is the correct,
intended behaviour for now: it means the build system probed the Wayland
development files, found them, and dispatched to a backend that has not landed.
It is not a build-system bug.


When is the Wayland arm selected?
---------------------------------

Only when X11 is absent from the build machine entirely. X11 and Wayland
development files coexist on most desktop systems, and DGL prefers X11 whenever
it is present, because hosts embed plugin UIs through the X11 embedding
contract (of the formats DPF targets, only CLAP has a Wayland window API) and
standalone builds work fine on a Wayland session via XWayland.

That policy is expressed in three places, which must be kept in agreement:

  * dgl/src/pugl.hpp      -- `#elif defined(HAVE_X11)` before
                             `#elif defined(HAVE_WAYLAND)`, plus the long
                             rationale comment. This is the canonical statement.
  * Makefile.base.mk      -- `DGL_BACKEND_WAYLAND`, set only when HAVE_WAYLAND
                             is true and HAVE_X11 is not. Only this variable
                             gates -DHAVE_WAYLAND and the Wayland libraries.
  * cmake/DPF-plugin.cmake -- the `if(X11_FOUND) ... else()` split inside
                             `dpf__add_dgl_system_libs`.

To exercise the Wayland arm on a machine that has X11 installed:

    make -C dgl HAVE_X11= HAVE_WAYLAND=true DGL_BACKEND_WAYLAND=true
    cmake -DCMAKE_DISABLE_FIND_PACKAGE_X11=ON ...


Build requirements already probed for
-------------------------------------

The build system treats the Wayland backend as all-or-nothing. HAVE_WAYLAND is
true only when every one of these is present:

  Libraries (pkg-config, probed together as HAVE_WAYLAND_LIBS):
    wayland-client, wayland-egl, wayland-cursor, xkbcommon, egl

  Tooling (probed together as HAVE_WAYLAND_TOOLS):
    wayland-protocols (pkg-config), wayland-scanner (in PATH)

`make features` prints all three so a partial installation is easy to diagnose.
The tooling half is needed because the xdg-shell protocol bindings are
generated at build time from the XML in wayland-protocols' datadir.


What Phase 4 adds
-----------------

Source files, mirroring the naming that pugl-extra already uses for the Haiku
and wasm backends, and matching the commented-out include block in pugl.cpp:

  wayland.h          shared internals: PuglWorldInternals / PuglInternals for
                     Wayland, registry and seat state, xkb keyboard state,
                     cursor theme handle
  wayland.c          core backend: registry bind, xdg-shell surface lifecycle,
                     frame callbacks, input (pointer / keyboard / touch),
                     clipboard, timers, the event loop feeding puglDispatchEvent
  wayland_stub.c     the no-graphics-API backend (PUGL_STUB), as x11_stub.c is
  wayland_cairo.c    Cairo backend over a wl_shm buffer pool
  wayland_gl.c       OpenGL backend over EGL + wl_egl_window

Generated at build time (hence the wayland-scanner requirement):

  xdg-shell-client-protocol.h / xdg-shell-protocol.c
  and likely xdg-decoration and viewporter alongside them

Phase 4 also replaces the #error in dgl/src/pugl.cpp with the real include
block, fills in the two stubs at the bottom of that file
(puglWaylandUpdateWithoutExposures, puglWaylandSetAppId), and adds the
DGL_USING_WAYLAND counterparts to the `#ifdef DGL_USING_X11` sites in
dgl/src/WindowPrivateData.cpp.

No further build-system work should be required: the probes, the gates, the
flag/library wiring and the preprocessor dispatch arms all already exist.
